---
const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Do I need a Cursor subscription?",
    a: "The catalog reflects what your Cursor account can use. Cursor subscription limits, model eligibility, and usage accounting still apply. Model Ferry only makes those models selectable in OpenCode."
  },
  {
    q: "Where is my Cursor credential stored?",
    a: "Model Ferry stores nothing. The browser sign-in mints a 90-day API key that the Cursor SDK keeps in ~/.cursor/sdk/auth.json, readable only by your user. It auto-renews before expiry. Set CURSOR_API_KEY to skip the browser flow."
  },
  {
    q: "Is Model Ferry affiliated with Cursor or OpenCode?",
    a: "No. Model Ferry is an independent open-source project built on Cursor's public Agent SDK. It is not affiliated with, sponsored, or endorsed by Anysphere, Inc. (Cursor) or Anomaly (OpenCode). Cursor and OpenCode are trademarks of their respective owners."
  },
  {
    q: "Which platforms does it support?",
    a: "macOS, Linux, and Windows 10/11. Use install.sh on macOS and Linux (launchd or systemd). Use install.ps1 in PowerShell on Windows (Task Scheduler). Manual npm setup works on all three."
  },
  {
    q: "Which clients does it support?",
    a: "OpenCode is the reference client, but the bridge speaks the standard OpenAI-compatible chat API over a loopback HTTP server, so any local client that supports custom providers can point at it."
  },
  {
    q: "Is anything sent off my machine?",
    a: "Only your model prompts. As with any Cursor agent run, prompts and tool activity go to Cursor's servers (Anysphere) for inference. The SDK client and agent loop run locally. The bridge has no telemetry, binds to 127.0.0.1, and uses a per-install bearer token. Cursor agents are limited to MCP so OpenCode keeps shell and file execution."
  },
  {
    q: "How does local auth work?",
    a: "Setup mints a random bearer token into ~/.config/modelferry/config.json and writes it into OpenCode's cursorapi provider as apiKey. Chat, models, catalog refresh, and setup-page auth or config changes all require that token."
  },
  {
    q: "What if the default port is taken?",
    a: "The bridge scans the next 19 ports, binds the first free one, and records the resolved port in its config so the OpenCode provider, onboarding page, and CLI all agree."
  }
];
---
<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="heading">Frequently asked questions.</h2>
    <div class="list">
      {faqs.map((item) => (
        <details class="item">
          <summary>{item.q}</summary>
          <p>{item.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .container.narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  .heading { font-size: clamp(30px, 4vw, 44px); margin-bottom: 32px; }
  .item { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .item:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .item summary { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; padding: 20px 0; font-weight: 600; font-size: 16px; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; }
  .item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .item summary::after { content: "+"; color: var(--faint); font-size: 20px; font-weight: 400; margin-left: 16px; }
  .item[open] summary::after { content: "–"; }
  .item p { margin: 0 0 20px; color: var(--muted); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; max-width: 620px; }
</style>
